<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/16th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Lepanto</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1571">1571</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-venetian-war-1570-1573">Ottoman–Venetian War (1570–1573)</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Lepanto was a naval battle fought on 7 October 1571 in the Gulf of Patras, off western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, between the fleet of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-league-1571">Holy League</a>, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-austria">Don John of Austria</a>, and the Ottoman fleet. The League, whose ships came chiefly from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and the Papal States, destroyed most of the Ottoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a>. It was the last great battle between galley fleets, though the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> soon rebuilt their navy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-lepanto.jpg" alt="Battle of Lepanto" width="320" height="197" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Laureys a Castro · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Laureys_a_Castro_-_The_Battle_of_Lepanto.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-v">Pope Pius V</a> promoted the Christian coalition to rescue the Venetian colony of Famagusta on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, which the Turks were besieging in early 1571. On 1 August the Venetians surrendered after being promised they could leave freely, but the Ottoman commander Lala Kara Mustafa Pasha broke his word, imprisoned them and had <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marco-antonio-bragadin">Marco Antonio Bragadin</a> flayed alive. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-league-1571">Holy League</a> included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a>, the Papal States, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> and other states. Its banner reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> on 14 August 1571, where it was consigned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-austria">John of Austria</a>, half-brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-spain">Philip II of Spain</a>, the overall commander.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-league-1571">Holy League</a> fleet had 206 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a> and six galleasses, large Venetian galleys with heavy artillery. The Ottoman admiral Ali Pasha commanded 211 war galleys and 63 galliots; the oarsmen were virtually all slaves, many of them captured Christians. The Christians had an estimated 1,815 guns against 750 Ottoman guns with insufficient ammunition, and their arquebusiers faced Ottoman composite bowmen.</p><p>On the morning of 7 October 1571 the Christian fleet formed up in four divisions on a north–south line, with the Venetian Agostino Barbarigo on the left, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-austria">John of Austria</a> in the centre and the Genoese Gianandrea Doria on the right, and met the Ottoman fleet in the Gulf of Patras. John of Austria had the galley slaves freed from their chains. The battle was in essence an infantry battle on floating platforms, and the Ottoman fleet was almost completely destroyed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>It was the largest naval battle in Western history since classical antiquity, with more than 450 warships, and the last major engagement fought almost entirely between rowing vessels. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, who had not lost a major naval battle since the fifteenth century, rebuilt quickly, with about 250 ships built within six months. The League failed to capitalise, since friction among the Christian leaders ended further operations and it regained none of the territories lost earlier. The loss of qualified crews led to a sharp decrease in Ottoman naval initiative after 1580.</p><p>The victory confirmed the de facto division of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> between an Ottoman east and a west under the Spanish Crown and its Italian allies. The League credited the victory to the Virgin Mary, and Pius V instituted the feast of Our Lady of Victory, now kept as the feast of Our Lady of the Rosary.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Lepanto” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Order of Saint Stephen in 1561, a crusading order of knights based at Pisa, whose galleys fought the Ottomans and the Barbary corsairs; Tuscan galleys served in the Christian fleet at the <b>Battle of Lepanto</b> in 1571, and in 1607 the knights sacked Bona on the Algerian coast.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-tuscany">Grand Duchy of Tuscany · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Mediterranean, galleys from Spain and its Italian kingdoms fought the Ottomans and Barbary corsairs, and a Holy League fleet under Don John of Austria won the <b>Battle of Lepanto</b> in 1571.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-spain">Habsburg Spain · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Spain the kingdom supplied soldiers, money and galleys for the Habsburg wars, including ships at the <b>Battle of Lepanto</b> in 1571, and viceroys built chains of coastal watchtowers against Barbary corsairs.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-naples">Kingdom of Naples · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Papal galleys under Marcantonio Colonna served at the <b>Battle of Lepanto</b> in 1571, and the War of Castro of 1641–1644 showed the limits of papal arms against small Italian princes.</blockquote><a href="/empire/papal-states">Papal States · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Uluj Ali, a Calabrian convert, became beylerbey in 1568, captured Tunis in 1569 and commanded the Ottoman left wing at the <b>Battle of Lepanto</b> in 1571.</blockquote><a href="/empire/regency-of-algiers">Regency of Algiers · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Against the Ottoman Empire it fought a series of wars from 1463, contributed about half the galleys of the Holy League at the <b>Battle of Lepanto</b> in 1571, defended Candia for over twenty years until 1669, and conquered the Morea under Francesco Morosini in 1684–1687.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-venice">Republic of Venice · Military</a></li></ul></section>
